Falsifies `paging::x86_64::no_encoded_leaf_is_both_writable_and_executable`.

The defect: `Ia32e::attrs` stops setting `XD` on a writable page, on the reasoning
that a data page is not code and nothing will jump into it. That reasoning is
exactly the one W^X exists to refuse, and the encoding is where it has to be
refused rather than argued: the portable `Flags` constructors already keep write
and execute apart, so a page reaching here writable and non-executable is a page
whose leaf must carry `XD`, or a buffer overflow on `user_data` gains an
instruction pointer.

Expected to fail: `assert!(leaf & XD != 0 || leaf & RW == 0)`, the harness's only
assertion, on `Flags::kernel_data()` and `Flags::user_data()`.

**It also turns `x86_64::the_leaf_keeps_address_and_permissions_apart` red, and
that is a property of the encoding rather than an accident.** `leaf_flags` reads a
clear `XD` with `US` set as user-executable, so a writable user page no longer
round-trips to the flags it was built from. Two harnesses catch one defect because
the same bit carries both claims; neither of them is redundant, since the
round-trip harness would stay green on a defect that set `XD` too often and this
one would not.

**Redone 2026-09-17 by milestone 313's audit, with the defect unchanged.** The audit added a
comment block above the `if` this patch edits (the `CR4.SMEP` caveat: x86 refuses a supervisor fetch
from a user page only while that bit is set, which the kernel now sets), so the record's context no
longer matched and `--affected-since` reported it stale, as it should. The one-line defect is the
same; only the lines around it moved. CI found it because `--affected-since` follows a diff into
`crates/`, and the lane's own `--sweep kernel` did not, which is recorded in the milestone's block.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/x86_64.rs
@@ -108,7 +108,7 @@
         // which `arch::x86_64::init` sets on every core that offers it; this crate cannot check.
         if flags.is_kernel_executable() {
             bits |= SW_KERNEL_EXEC;
-        } else if !flags.is_user_executable() {
+        } else if !flags.is_user_executable() && !flags.is_writable() {
             bits |= XD;
         }
         bits
